etru-rares"><span class="enc-name">Petru Rareș</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Petru Rareș was Prince of Moldavia from 1527 to 1538 and again from 1541 to 1546, an illegitimate son of Stephen the Great.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/petsamo"><span class="enc-name">Petsamo</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Petsamo is a region on the Arctic coast near the Norwegian border, now the Pechengsky district of Russia's Murmansk Oblast.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/petuaria"><span class="enc-name">Petuaria</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Petuaria was a Roman town at Brough on the Humber in the East Riding of Yorkshire, England, listed by Ptolemy as the town of the Parisi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/petun"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/petun.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Petun</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Petun, or Tionontati, were an Iroquoian people living south of Georgian Bay in what is now Ontario, Canada, neighbours and close allies of the Wendat.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/peuce"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/peuce.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Peuce</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Peuce was an island in the Danube delta known to Greek and Roman writers, named after its pine trees and lying between the river's arms in modern Romania.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/peucetius"><span class="enc-name">Peucetius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Peucetius is a figure of Greek legend, a son of the Arcadian king Lycaon, said to have led Arcadian settlers to Italy with his brother Oenotrus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/peugeot"><span class="enc-name">Peugeot</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Peugeot is a French car maker founded by an industrial family from the Montbéliard area, Lutherans like most of the region's people.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pevensey-castle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pevensey-castle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pevensey Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pevensey Castle is a medieval castle in East Sussex, England, built inside the walls of the Roman fort of Anderitum.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pfaffenbrief"><span class="enc-name">Pfaffenbrief</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pfaffenbrief, or Priests' Charter, was an agreement of 1370 between Zürich, Lucerne, Zug, Uri, Schwyz and Unterwalden.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pfalzgrafenstein-castle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pfalzgrafenstein-castle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pfalzgrafenstein Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Pfalzgrafenstein Castle is a toll castle on a small island in the Rhine near Kaub, Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pforzheim"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pforzheim.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pforzheim</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pforzheim is a city at the northern edge of the Black Forest in Baden-Württemberg, Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pha-mueang"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pha-mueang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pha Mueang</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pha Mueang, or Pho Khun Pha Mueang, was a Tai chief of Mueang Rat in north-central Thailand in the thirteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pha-that-luang"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pha-that-luang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pha That Luang</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Pha That Luang is a large gold-covered Buddhist stupa in Vientiane, Laos, built by King Setthathirath around 1566 on the site of an older shrine.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/phad-painting"><span class="enc-name">Phad painting</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Phad painting is a folk tradition of Rajasthan in which long cloth scrolls are painted with the stories of local hero-deities such as Pabuji and Devnarayan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/phaenomena"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/phaenomena.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Phaenomena</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Phaenomena is a didactic poem by Aratus of Soli, written around 275 BCE at the court of Antigonus II Gonatas.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/phags-pa-script"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/phags-pa-script.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">ʼPhags-pa script</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The ʼPhags-pa script is an alphabet created by the Tibetan lama Drogön Chögyal Phagpa at the request of Kublai Khan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/phaistos"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/phaistos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Phaistos</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Phaistos is a Bronze Age archaeological site in south-central Crete, Greece, with the remains of a Minoan palace and the town around it, one of the largest cities of…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/phaistos-disc"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/phaistos-disc.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Phaistos Disc</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Phaistos Disc is a clay disc found by the Italian archaeologist Luigi Pernier in the Minoan palace of Phaistos on Crete in 1908.</span></a></li></ul>
